## Test Maintainability Analysis

### Summary

Your `InputValidatorTests` class has **9 test methods** with two clear maintainability patterns:

---

### Finding 1: Copy-Paste Test Methods (Category 3)

All 9 methods share identical structure, differing only in input and expected output. They split into two groups:

**Invalid-input group** (5 methods): `Validate_EmptyString_ReturnsFalse`, `Validate_TooShort_ReturnsFalse`, `Validate_TooLong_ReturnsFalse`, `Validate_ContainsSpecialChars_ReturnsFalse`, `Validate_Null_ReturnsFalse`, `Validate_WhitespaceOnly_ReturnsFalse`

**Valid-input group** (3 methods): `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsTrue`, `Validate_MinLength_ReturnsTrue`, `Validate_MaxLength_ReturnsTrue`

**Suggested refactoring — convert to `[DataRow]` parameterized tests:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;

namespace Validation.Tests;

[TestClass]
public sealed class InputValidatorTests
{
    private InputValidator _validator = null!;

    [TestInitialize]
    public void Setup() => _validator = new InputValidator();

    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow(null, "Input cannot be empty", DisplayName = "Null input")]
    [DataRow("", "Input cannot be empty", DisplayName = "Empty string")]
    [DataRow("   ", "Input cannot be empty", DisplayName = "Whitespace only")]
    [DataRow("ab", "Input must be at least 3 characters", DisplayName = "Too short")]
    [DataRow("hello<script>", "Input contains invalid characters", DisplayName = "Special chars")]
    public void Validate_InvalidInput_ReturnsFalse(string? input, string expectedError)
    {
        var result = _validator.Validate(input);
        Assert.IsFalse(result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
    }

    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow("abc", DisplayName = "Min length (3)")]
    [DataRow("hello-world", DisplayName = "Typical valid input")]
    public void Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsTrue(string input)
    {
        var result = _validator.Validate(input);
        Assert.IsTrue(result.IsValid);
        Assert.IsNull(result.ErrorMessage);
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void Validate_TooLong_ReturnsFalse()
    {
        // DataRow can't hold computed values, so this stays separate
        var result = _validator.Validate(new string('x', 256));
        Assert.IsFalse(result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual("Input must not exceed 255 characters", result.ErrorMessage);
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void Validate_MaxLength_ReturnsTrue()
    {
        var result = _validator.Validate(new string('a', 255));
        Assert.IsTrue(result.IsValid);
        Assert.IsNull(result.ErrorMessage);
    }
}
```

**Impact:** 9 methods → 4 methods. Adding a new invalid-input case is now a single `[DataRow]` line instead of a 7-line method.

---

### Finding 2: Repeated Object Construction (Category 1)

`new InputValidator()` appears in all 9 methods identically. Extracted into `[TestInitialize]` above.

---

### Trade-offs

| Gain | Cost |
|------|------|
| Adding a case = 1 line instead of 7 | Failure messages are slightly less obvious at a glance (mitigated by `DisplayName`) |
| Single assertion block to maintain | Computed values (`new string(...)`) can't go in `[DataRow]`, so 2 methods stay separate |
| Shared construction reduces noise | `[TestInitialize]` adds mild coupling (acceptable for a stateless validator) |

### Priority

This is a **high-value, low-risk** refactoring — the tests are simple, inputs are compile-time constants, and the pattern is extremely repetitive. Would you like me to apply this refactoring?
